volume>11</volume>:<fpage>241</fpage>&#x02013;253.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10637305</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="journal">Slep, K.C., S.L. Rogers, S.L. Elliott, H. Ohkura, P.A. Kolodziej, and R.D. Vale. <year>2005</year>. Structural determinants for EB1-mediated recruitment of APC and spectraplakins to the microtubule plus end. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>168</volume>:<fpage>587</fpage>&#x02013;598.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15699215</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><citation citation-type="journal">Surrey, T., F. Nedelec, S. Leibler, and E. Karsenti. <year>2001</year>. Physical properties determining self-organization of motors and microtubules. <source>Science.</source> <volume>292</volume>:<fpage>1167</fpage>&#x02013;1171.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11349149</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><citation citation-type="journal">Tulu, U.S., N.M. Rusan, and P. Wadsworth. <year>2003</year>. Peripheral, non-centrosome-associated microtubules contribute to spindle formation in centrosome-containing cells. <source>Curr. Biol.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>1894</fpage>&#x02013;1899.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14588246</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><citation citation-type="journal">Wadsworth, P., and A. Khodjakov. <year>2004</year>. E pluribus unum: towards a universal mechanism for spindle assembly. <source>Trends Cell Biol.</source> <volume>14</volume>:<fpage>413</fpage>&#x02013;419.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15308207</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib35"><citation citation-type="journal">Wakefield, J.G., S. Bonaccorsi, and M. Gatti. <year>2001</year>. The <italic>Drosophila</italic> protein asp is involved in microtubule organization during spindle formation and cytokinesis. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>153</volume>:<fpage>637</fpage>&#x02013;648.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11352927</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib36"><citation citation-type="journal">Walczak, C.E., S. Verma,